# Shipped class-cast library for the conversion editor.
# Each 'cast <Name> ... end' block defines a class-to-class cast the DSL names as the
# '#link To <- From : <Name>' suffix. The editor uses name/accepts/yields.
# A block with a 'value <transform>' line is a VALUE cast instead (1.27.0, see
# StripDB below): a built-in string transform, not a class cast.
#
# The realization hints (dfm/compat/pas/todo) ARE CONSUMED by the engine as of
# 2026-09-16 (row 6): `dfm keep-bytes-if-compatible` carries a streamed payload
# to the target path when its format is in `compat`, emitting `todo` when it is
# not. From 2026-09-16 until 1.27.0 convert-apply also RENDERED `pas` at a .pas
# access site; 1.27.0 RETIRED that, and today `pas` only tells convert-apply the
# cast was found (an empty one says the library has no .pas recipe). Why it was
# retired: a `pas` template is a STATEMENT (dst.Assign(src);) and an access site
# is a member token INSIDE an expression, so no splice is correct -- the 1.26.x
# rendering wrote code that did not parse. Every such site is now refused one
# by one (cast-not-applied), convert it by hand; see the paragraph at the end.
#
# THE HISTORY HERE IS DELIBERATE, and please keep it. Between 2026-09-04 and
# 2026-09-16 this paragraph read "EDITOR-SIDE ONLY ... nothing under srceport\ or
# src\cli\ reads a .castlib", and it said so because it was CORRECTING an earlier
# header that had claimed a consumer which did not exist. Spec item G6.3 asked
# for that comment to be corrected or the consumer built; the comment was
# corrected first, and the consumer has now been built. A bare "consumed by
# convert-apply" would erase the fact that this file once described a consumer
# that was not there -- which is the exact failure the note was guarding against.
#
# The payload format is sniffed from the BYTES, not inferred from the property
# name: a .dfm blob is a streamed TPicture (a length-prefixed class name, then
# that graphic's own framing -- a size for TBitmap -- then the image), so the
# wrapper class is read first and the image magic is the fallback.
#
# THE BYTES ARE RE-FRAMED, NOT COPIED (1.26.2). Each graphic class streams its
# Data in its own FILER FRAMING, and the two sides of a cast rarely agree:
# TPicture.Data is 07 'TBitmap' <Int32 size> BM..., TdxSmartGlyph.Data is the
# bare BM... file. Carrying the TPicture bytes verbatim (1.25.2) produced glyphs
# that loaded as text and raised 'Unsupported image format.' when read. The
# engine unwraps the source (TPicture / TBitmap / bare image -- VCL framing,
# fixed and known) and re-frames it for the TARGET, which it takes from:
#   dfmdata graphic|bitmap|metafile|picture   -- declared here, or else
#   the single `yields` class, when it is a VCL graphic (TPicture, TBitmap,
#   TJPEGImage, TMetafile, TIcon, TPngImage, TGIFImage, TWICImage).
#   graphic  = the bare image file (TGraphic.ReadData: TIcon, TPngImage,
#              TGIFImage, TWICImage, TdxSmartImage, TdxSmartGlyph)
#   bitmap   = [Int32 size][image] (TBitmap, TJPEGImage)
#   metafile = [Int32 size counting itself][image] (TMetafile)
#   picture  = TPicture: [len][class name][that class's framing]
# A third-party yields class needs `dfmdata`: the engine does not guess whether
# a class it never read overrides ReadData. Nothing is carried -- the instance
# is reported with the reason and `todo` -- when the source wrapper names an
# unknown graphic class, its size field disagrees with its bytes, the image
# format is not in `compat`, or the target framing is unknown.
#
# Since 2026-09-04 the engine DOES split the ': <Name>' suffix off the #link FromPath
# (it used to swallow it and then report the valid line as a missing path), and
# convert-apply refuses to rewrite a .pas access site for a link carrying a CLASS
# cast rather than renaming without performing the conversion (since 1.27.0 even
# when the cast has a `pas` template: a statement cannot be spliced into an
# expression -- each site is reported, convert it by hand).
# Strict 7-bit ASCII, CRLF.

cast AssignGraphic
  accepts TPicture, TBitmap, TGraphic, TPngImage, TIcon
  yields  TdxSmartGlyph
  dfm     keep-bytes-if-compatible
  dfmdata graphic
  compat  png, bmp
  pas     '{dst}.Assign({src});'
  todo    'transfer image from {src} by hand (TBitmap/TPicture -> TdxSmartGlyph)'
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VALUE casts (1.27.0). A `cast` block with a `value <transform>` line names a
# transform BUILT INTO the engine that rewrites a linked STRING value -- not a
# class-to-class cast, so it has no `pas` template and its .pas sites are not
# refused the way a class cast's are. Known transforms (an unknown one is a
# load error; convert-apply fails the unit rather than not perform the cast):
#   strip-db  removes ONE trailing '.db', any case: 'MET1.db' -> 'MET1',
#             'X.DB' -> 'X'; 'A.dbf', 'A.db.bak', 'MET1' and '' are unchanged.
# Applied to the .dfm value of each converted instance, and in the .pas only
# to `<instance>.<Prop> := '<one literal>';` -- any other right-hand side and
# every read is reported for checking by hand, never edited. No parameters,
# no patterns: one fixed, named transform per keyword.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Accepts is an EXACT type name (no alias walk), so the string alias TFileName is listed
# beside string (owner ruling 2026-10-19); add another alias here the same way.
cast StripDB
  accepts string, TFileName
  yields  string
  value   strip-db
  todo    'TableName kept its .db where it was not one string literal -- check by hand'
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ENUM casts. Named by the same '#link To <- From : <Name>' suffix as a class
# cast, and EXECUTED by the engine: convert-apply/convert-reemit translate the
# .dfm value member by member when the rule book names the cast and the run is
# given --castlib.
#
# Grammar, chosen to mirror the two DSLs it sits between so there is nothing new
# to learn: 'from'/'to' match '#mapping <Name> from <T> to <T>', the '->' in a
# 'map' line matches a '#when ... -> ...' branch, and 'else' matches '#else'.
# One pair per line, so the file stays diffable and a tool can append a line
# without rewriting one.
#
# MATCHING IS BY NAME, NOT ORDINAL. Two enums being converted are different
# types whose members correspond by MEANING; equal ordinals across unrelated
# types are a coincidence, not evidence.
#
# Without an 'else', a value no 'map' line covers is NOT written and is reported
# as `enum-cast-unmapped`. That is deliberate: the target is a different enum
# type, so passing the source member name through either fails to load or
# quietly means something else.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

enum ButtonLayout
  from Vcl.Buttons.TButtonLayout
  to   dxCore.TdxButtonLayout
  map  blGlyphLeft -> dxblGlyphLeft
  map  blGlyphRight -> dxblGlyphRight
  map  blGlyphTop -> dxblGlyphTop
  map  blGlyphBottom -> dxblGlyphBottom
  todo 'verify the four positions against the target control at runtime'
end
